<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">History of the Haudenosaunee</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as History of the Iroquois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The history of the Haudenosaunee, or Iroquois, goes back to the formation of their league, which many scholars date to about 1450, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a>, Cayuga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">the Peacemaker</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law of Peace</a>; the Tuscarora joined in the 18th century. A Council of Fifty sought consensus, clan mothers held considerable power, and captives of mourning wars were adopted to replace the dead or enslaved. From 1609 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> brought the defeat and absorption of the Huron, Neutral and Erie. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> split the confederacy, and after it much of its homeland was ceded in treaties of 1784 and 1794, while many followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a> to the Grand River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/history-of-the-haudenosaunee.jpg" alt="History of the Haudenosaunee" width="330" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hale, Horatio (collector) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiefs_of_the_Six_Nations_at_Brantford,_Canada,_explaining_their_wampum_belts_to_Horatio_Hale_September_14,_1871.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
